course: politics
lesson: 15
title: Grandma and Uncle on opposite sides: staying friends
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Big family dinners are where politics often explodes: everyone is at one table, everyone has views, and the turkey is getting cold. Grandma and Uncle can love each other and still vote differently.
explain: People see politics differently partly because they grew up in different times and lived different lives. Grandad's past and your cousin's future both matter, which is why they can look at the same news and see different things.
explain: There are good tricks for keeping the peace: change the subject ("Who wants pie?"), say the other side's best reason before you argue, correct a wrong fact kindly and in private, mute the group chat, or agree to disagree.
explain: Understanding someone is not the same as agreeing with them, and "I don't know yet" is an honest answer at any age.
explain: Families say it their own way: at a Pittsburgh dinner it is "yinz want more pie?" (yinz = you all), down South it is "y'all", in Sligo a cousin might call Uncle an "eejit" (a fool, said half in fun) and then the craic (fun) carries on, and in Liverpool "our kid" means your brother or sister.
fact: People who love each other can still vote differently, and big holidays often bring political arguments to the table.
fact: Polarisation is when people split into two sides that hardly talk to each other; common ground is what both sides agree on.
fact: Different generations grew up in different times; news reports said older and younger people often voted very differently, for example in the Brexit vote of 2016, when the UK voted on leaving the European Union.
fact: Changing the subject can calm an argument; it does not mean the argument is over, only that dinner is not the time for it.
fact: Kids often repeat what they hear at home; friends can stay friends even when their parents vote differently.
fact: Sometimes political arguments make relatives fall out and stop talking; many families reconcile, often by agreeing to talk about other things.
fact: Children cannot vote yet, and being undecided is fine; people make up their minds by learning and listening over many years.
fact: A steelman means describing the other side's view in its strongest form; it helps you understand without making you agree.
fact: People of any age can believe false things they see online; correcting someone in front of others can embarrass them, and embarrassed people often dig in.
fact: People type things in group chats they would not say face to face; muting a chat is a way to take a break.
fact: Many couples and friends disagree about politics and still love each other; they agree to disagree.
fact: In a referendum everyone votes yes or no on one question, such as changing Ireland's constitution, the country's main set of rules.
fact: People disagree whether families should talk politics at holidays or keep them politics-free.
exercise: Can people who love each other vote differently? | Yes, and they can still love each other | No, families must all vote the same | Only if they live apart | Only in some countries
exercise: Why do political arguments often happen at holiday dinners? | Families come together and everyone has views | Holidays are when all the votes in the country are counted | Politicians visit every home | It is a holiday tradition by law
exercise: What is polarisation? | When people split into two sides that hardly talk | When everyone agrees about everything and nobody argues | When the North Pole melts | When a party wins every seat
exercise: What is common ground? | Things both sides agree on | A field for football | A party's slogan | A type of vote
exercise: Why might Grandad and a teenager see politics differently? | They grew up in different times | Teenagers cannot read the news yet | Grandads are always right about everything | They live on two different planets
exercise: What does the aunt do when she asks "Who wants pie?" | Changes the subject to calm things down | Ends the argument forever | Takes the uncle's side | Starts a new argument
exercise: Mia's friend says, "My mum says your mum is wrong." Can the girls still be friends? | Yes, your friend is not your friend's parents | No, they must pick sides now and stop playing | Only if their two mums agree about the election | Only after the election is completely over and done
exercise: What does it mean to reconcile? | To make up after falling out | To vote twice | To change your party | To stop eating together
exercise: What does undecided mean? | Not having made up your mind yet | Having voted already | Refusing to listen | Agreeing with Grandma
exercise: What is a steelman? | Describing the other side's view in its strongest form | A very strong politician | A metal statue in parliament | Ignoring the other side and repeating your own view louder and louder
exercise: Does saying the other side's best reason mean you agree with it? | No, understanding is not the same as agreeing | Yes, you must switch sides and vote their way | Yes, it counts as a vote for the other side | No, it means you think they are stupid and wrong
exercise: Grandad repeats something false from social media. What usually works better? | Correcting the fact gently, later and in private | Laughing at him in front of everyone, so he learns his lesson and never posts again | Saying nothing ever | Posting about him online
exercise: Why do group chats turn into arguments fast? | People type things they would not say face to face | Phones make people angry, because the screens give off waves | Group chats count votes | Only uncles use them
exercise: What does it mean to mute a chat? | To turn off its messages for a while | To delete the family | To send an angry reply | To report it to the police
exercise: What is a referendum? | A vote where everyone answers one yes or no question | A family dinner | A debate on TV between the leaders of all the parties before an election | A party's list of promises
exercise: What is a constitution? | The country's main set of rules | A family argument | A kind of election poster | A health service
test: "Back in my day things were better" says Grandad in Kent. His cousin says "Not for everyone, Grandad." Why can both feel right? | They grew up in different times and lived different lives | One of them must be lying, because only one of them can be right about the past | Grandad has forgotten everything | Teenagers know nothing about the past
test: «yinz are gonna fight about the election again?? who wants pie» says Aunt Pat (yinz = you all). Why does the pie line work? | It changes the subject so dinner can go on | Pie settles who is right about the election | It ends the argument forever, for good and all | It makes Uncle switch parties for a slice of pie
test: «my bestie said ur mum is wrong about the election, I'm lowkey hurt» (lowkey = a bit, quietly). What is the best way to see it? | Kids repeat what they hear at home, and friends can stay friends | She is no longer a friend and Mia should find a new bestie who agrees with her mum | Mia must argue for her mum | Mia's mum must change her vote
test: Grandma and Uncle have not spoken for months after a politics row. What would happen if they agreed to talk about other things? | They might make up and the family barbecues could come back | They would have to vote the same way from now on, or the talking would not count | The election would be cancelled | Nothing could ever change
test: Grandma in Sligo asks Aisha who she would vote for. Aisha says "I dunno yet." Why is that a good answer? | It is honest; people make up their minds over many years | Children must always pick a side, so Aisha should choose the one Grandma likes | It shows she does not care | She is not allowed to speak
test: The cousins in Napier must say the other side's best reason before arguing. Your brother says that is "giving in". What is true? | Understanding someone is not the same as agreeing | It means you have lost the argument before you start | It is cheating at the dinner table, like peeking at cards | It only works with grandmas who are very, very patient
test: "Grandad's posting proper nonsense again, I'm gonna call him out in front of everyone" says Priya. What is the kinder, better plan? | Check the fact, then tell him gently and in private | Shout it at dinner so all can hear, so that Grandad feels ashamed and stops posting | Post a mean meme about him | Never speak to him again
test: The family chat explodes after Uncle's political post. "Bruv, I'm done" types Noah's cousin (bruv = mate, brother). What did Mum do that works? | Muted the chat to take a break | Left the family | Posted a bigger argument | Deleted Uncle's phone
test: Chloe's parents vote for different parties. "How do you stay married?" What is the answer? | They talk, listen and sometimes agree to disagree | They never talk about anything at all, not even dinner | One of them secretly votes the same | They argue every day
test: In Ireland's referendum, Grandma voted No and Uncle voted Yes. "Sure, they're still hugging, the eejits" laughs Oisin (eejit = fool, said fondly). What does this show? | You can love someone and still vote differently | The vote was cancelled | Referendums make families fall out forever, so they will stop hugging soon | Grandma changed her vote after
test: Some people say some beliefs are too important to pretend to agree about. What do others say? | No election is worth losing your family over | Families should never talk to each other again | Beliefs never matter in the end, not even a little | Only the loudest belief counts at a family dinner
exam: Identify the term which denotes the division of a population into two opposing camps which seldom communicate with one another. | Polarisation | Common ground | Reconciliation | Consensus-building
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice described as "steelmanning"? | The presentation of an opposing view in its strongest form, which does not entail agreement with it | The adoption of the opposing view as one's own | The refusal to listen to an opposing view | The presentation of an opposing view in its weakest form, in order that it may be defeated with the least effort
exam: Select the response consistent with the established guidance concerning the correction of false information repeated by an elderly relative. | The correction shall be made courteously and, where appropriate, in private | The correction shall be made publicly, before the assembled family, in order to embarrass the relative | No correction shall ever be attempted | The relative shall be excluded from family gatherings
exam: It is given that a referendum is held in respect of an amendment to the constitution of Ireland. Determine the nature of such a vote. | A vote in which the electorate answers a single question with yes or no | A vote in which members of parliament alone elect a prime minister from among their number | A vote in which each family casts one ballot | A vote in which candidates are ranked in order of preference
